{"id": "reasoning-6cc5948e-b845-5a53-a310-7a733b7f1fef-0", "sessionId": "2239200c-e579-08ab-66f6-b36b07b431f6", "turnId": "turn-647faa3ec979716f36b5c4d474cd829f", "createdAt": 1792489211359, "updatedAt": 1792489211359, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Budget is fresh (0 used). Now I have everything I need. Let me set up a todo list and start implementing.\n\nPlan:\n1. Extend `examples/beam_shell_coupling.py`:\n   - Add plastic DC constants: DC_TARGET=-11/60, DC_FULL_STEPS=11, DC_PREFIX_TARGET=-0.10, DC_PREFIX_STEPS=6, DC_RESUME_STEPS=5, DC_MAX_ITER=40, DC_TOL=1e-8 (TOLERANCE already 1e-8).\n   - Add `run_plastic(coupling, *, stage='full', restart_checkpoint=None, checkpoint_out=None, cancellation_token=None, progress_callback=None)` returning truthful JSON payload with requested/achieved displacement/control error.\n   - CLI: `--case preyield|plastic` (default preyield), `--stage full|prefix` (default full), `--restart PATH`, `--checkpoint-out PATH`.\n   - Flag validation before solve: `--stage/--restart/--checkpoint-out` only valid with `--case plastic`; `--restart` incompatible with `--stage prefix` (restart implies resume to full target); maybe `--restart` with `--stage full` is the resume. Actually design: `--restart PATH` implies resume stage (5 increments to -11/60). Should `--restart` + `--stage full` be allowed? The brief says stage full|prefix default full; resume is via --restart. I'll make `--restart` require `--stage full` (default) and reject `--restart --stage prefix`. Also reject plastic-case flags with preyield case.\n   - Checkpoint written only on completed target: `result.restart_checkpoint_bytes()` written to `--checkpoint-out` path. On cancellation/failure, do not overwrite existing checkpoint file (don't write at all).\n   - Cancellation: observe public `event['type']` (the emit_progress presumably passes a dict with 'type' key — the existing `_AcceptedHistory` uses `event.get(\"type\")`). For DC events, type is also \"nonlinear_static_step\" with control=\"displacement\". Track accepted increments, last control_value/load_factor.\n   - Failed-terminal restart refusal: when `--restart` points to a checkpoint whose terminal_status != completed, solve raises ValueError(\"restart checkpoint does not end at a continuable completed static target\") — report truthfully with nonzero exit. How to detect? The ValueError propagates from solve_static_nonlinear. In CLI, catch ValueError from restart? Hmm — \"other exceptions keep tracebacks\" was slice1 design. For restart refusal, we should report truthfully with nonzero exit. I'll catch the specific ValueError in run_plastic when restart is provided and return a payload with status \"restart_refused\", stop_reason=str(exc), completed False. Actually careful: catching ValueError broadly could mask other errors. The refusal message is specific: \"restart checkpoint does not end at a continuable completed static target\". I could catch ValueError and check... but other ValueErrors (e.g. fingerprint mismatch → NonlinearCheckpointError, which is a different class?). Let me check NonlinearCheckpointError's base. Actually, for a failed-terminal checkpoint, load_nonlinear_checkpoint succeeds (hash valid), then validate succeeds, then _restore_static_path_state raises ValueError at terminal_status check. For a corrupted checkpoint, NonlinearCheckpointError. I'll catch ValueError only around the solve when restart_checkpoint is not None, and report status \"restart_refused\" with the message. That's truthful.\n\n   Wait — but also \"invalidflagsbefore solve\" — flag validation before any solve. And \"cancellation/failurepreserveinputcheckpoint\" — the input checkpoint file bytes must not be modified by the run.\n\n   For the plastic payload JSON: add requested/achieved displacement/control error. Fields: coupling, case='plastic', status, stop_reason, requested_control (target displacement), achieved_control (probe uz at e